module poisoned_recv_match.neg_1

// ONE SEEDED MISTAKE: `mystery` is not a callable novac knows. Exactly one
// diagnostic may come out, and it must be that one -- never an internal
// compiler error.
//
// WHAT THIS CELL GUARDS. When an initializer is refused, `binds.nv` still binds
// the name, with `no_ty()`, so later uses raise no second complaint. A METHOD
// CALL on such a name is then never decided -- the judge has nothing to look a
// method up on -- and `type_of` used to read that silence as a violation of the
// pass order and die.
//
// WHY THE ARMS ARE QUALIFIED, and it is not decoration. Until 2026-09-20 the
// parser stopped at `Kind.A =>` altogether, so the walk never reached the
// method call and the hole stayed invisible. Reading the form opened the path,
// and `check/check.nv` began to ice where it had produced diagnostics. Both
// halves are needed: a match whose arms are qualified, and a method call on a
// receiver whose type never arrived. Neither alone reproduces, which is why
// this file carries both.
//
// THE PASS-ORDER CHECK ITSELF STAYS ALIVE. It catches a real class -- two
// answers to one question, plan 274.4 -- and the fix gave it a distinguishing
// question rather than removing it: is the receiver typed at all. A cell that
// merely asserted "no ice" would pass just as well with the check deleted, so
// the property here is "ONE named diagnostic", which deleting it would break in
// the other direction.

type Kind enum A | B

fn pick(k Kind, n int) -> int {
    ro s = mystery(n)
    match k {
        Kind.A => s.byte_len()
        Kind.B => 0
    }
}
